course: dictionary
lesson: 13951
title: Words for freedom
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Liberation comes from the verb liberate, which means to set free. A liberation is the moment when people who were controlled, trapped or imprisoned are set free. «The town held a parade to celebrate its liberation after the war ended.» The clue is the parade: people were happy because they were free again.
explain: Liberation can also mean gaining equal rights and freedom for a whole group of people. «Students joined the movement for liberation and equal rights.» Here the word is about fairness for many people, not one place. Ask yourself: is someone being set free from control, or from unfair treatment?
explain: Sometimes the word describes a feeling. «Finishing the exams was a liberation!» Nobody was locked up, but the writer felt free from worries and rules. Read the whole sentence to find out which meaning fits.
fact: Liberation is a noun, and it is usually uncountable: you say "the liberation", not "a liberations".
fact: «Liberation» means setting people or a place free from someone who controlled or imprisoned them.
fact: «Liberation» can mean gaining equal rights and freedom for a group of people.
fact: «Liberation» can also mean a feeling of being free from worries or rules.
fact: The verb «liberate» means to set free, so liberation is the act of setting free.
exercise: «The town celebrated its liberation after the enemy army left.» What does liberation mean here? | A new road built through the hills | A school trip to the seaside | *Being set free from control by an enemy | A long, quiet holiday
exercise: In «the movement for liberation and equal rights», what does liberation mean? | *Gaining freedom and equal rights for a group of people | A type of weather with lots of rain | Buying new clothes for a big party
exercise: Which sentence uses liberation to mean a feeling of freedom from worries? | Liberation day is held every spring | The army marched into the city at dawn | The guards opened the gate for the liberation of the captives | *«Finishing the exams was a liberation!»
test: «The prisoners cheered on the day of their liberation.» What does liberation mean? | *Being set free from a prison | Playing a game in the yard | Losing their way home
test: Which word is closest in meaning to liberation, as in being set free? | argument | *release | sleep | hunger
exam: Jayden says liberation means being trapped. Is he right? | Yes, liberation means being trapped behind walls | *No, liberation means being set free | Yes, liberation means a loud argument | No, liberation means buying a ticket
